# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
policy_module(ai_tools_memfdexec, 1.0.0)

# ai_tools OPTIONAL GROUP: memfdexec  [DEFAULT: DISABLED]
#
# Grants ai_tools_t map + execute on the anonymous in-memory files it creates itself, so a runtime may execute code it
# generated without writing that code to disk. .NET is the toolchain that needs it: it creates an executable `apphost`
# and runs its W^X JIT by writing generated native code to a memfd_create(2) file and mapping it a second time
# PROT_EXEC. The core module grants `execmem` (anonymous read-write-execute pages, shared with V8's JIT) but NOT
# `execute` on a FILE mapping, so that second mapping fails EACCES and any project that builds or runs a native host
# aborts:
#   - executable output types: console apps, ASP.NET Core, worker/hosted services
#   - test runners with an out-of-process host: xunit.v3, single-file publish
# Library builds and in-process test runners (MSTest on Microsoft.Testing.Platform) do NOT need it, which is why this is
# a separate group from tmpmap. The group is named for the capability rather than for .NET: any W^X JIT emitting
# into a memfd -- a Wasm runtime, LuaJIT, a JVM under some flags -- needs exactly this grant and no more.
#
# THE TYPE IS THIS MODULE'S OWN. tmpfs is declared `fs_use_trans`, so an inode on the internal shmem mount
# that memfd_create(2) allocates takes the type a type_transition names between the creating domain and the filesystem's
# SID; with no such rule it takes the filesystem's own type, tmpfs_t, which every tmpfs file on the host shares.
# The type_transition below gives this domain's memfds a private type, so the execute grant reaches the files this
# domain created and no others -- a memfd received from another domain over SCM_RIGHTS keeps tmpfs_t and stays
# non-executable here.
#
# SECURITY: this permits fileless in-memory execution (memfd + PROT_EXEC, the classic evasion primitive), but it does
# not grant a new PRIVILEGE. The domain already holds `execmem`, so in-process arbitrary native execution is already
# possible; this only extends it to a file-backed executable mapping. Code executed this way still runs as ai_tools_t --
# there is no entrypoint from this domain to a more privileged one -- so an in-memory payload lands back in the same DAC
# + type confinement, with the handback boundary and no-sudo posture intact. It is off by default because it widens
# the surface (and detectability) around in-memory execution.
#
# SCOPE: this grant does NOT touch ai_tools_tmp_t, the on-disk /tmp type. tmpmap adds `map` there and never `execute`;
# /tmp stays mounted noexec, which no rule here changes. It is NOT `execmod`: .NET's W^X JIT uses two separate mappings
# (one writable, one executable) of the same memfd, so it needs { map execute }, never a writable-and-executable single
# mapping.
#
# SECCOMP: memfd_create passes the session's @system-service SystemCallFilter (only namespace syscalls are filtered),
# so SELinux is the operative gate for this path.
#
# The permission set is the minimum an enforcing bring-up observes for that sequence, and nothing here is speculative.
# A transition that does not fire leaves every memfd tmpfs_t and breaks the workload rather than widening it,
# so `avc-testsuite.sh` reads the live label of a memfd back through /proc/self/fd and fails visibly on the shared type.

require {
    type ai_tools_t;
    type tmpfs_t;
}

type ai_tools_memfd_t;
files_tmpfs_file(ai_tools_memfd_t)

# An anonymous memfd this domain creates is born ai_tools_memfd_t instead of the host-shared tmpfs_t.
type_transition ai_tools_t tmpfs_t:file ai_tools_memfd_t;

# memfd double-mapped W^X JIT / apphost: size and write the generated code through a read-write mapping of the memfd,
# then execute it from a second mapping. The executable mapping needs `execute`; both mappings need `map`; creating,
# sizing and writing the memfd needs open/read/write/getattr.
allow ai_tools_t ai_tools_memfd_t:file { open read write getattr map execute };
